This produces rtl-for-vs-code-and-cursor-agents-<version>.vsix.
Give it to the user to test. Remind them of the Custom CSS reload cycle if they say it didn't work.
Once user confirms working — commit, push to both branches, release.
Commit + push to both branches (PowerShell)
# On main: commit the fix and the version bump as SEPARATE commits
# (keeps the fix cherry-pickable cleanly to feat/cursor-rtl-support)
git checkout main
git add rtl-for-vs-code-agents.js  # or whichever files
git commit -F .git/COMMIT_FIX.txt   # write the message to a tempfile first
$fixCommit = git rev-parse HEAD
git add package.json README.md     # README changelog on main only
git commit -F .git/COMMIT_VERSION.txt
git push fork main

# Cherry-pick ONLY the fix commit to the PR branch (NOT the version bump,
# NOT any README/rebranding/rtl-config.js changes)
git checkout feat/cursor-rtl-support
git cherry-pick $fixCommit
git push fork feat/cursor-rtl-support
git checkout main
If git push returns Internal Server Error from GitHub — it's a transient GitHub issue, just retry the push.
If the cherry-pick touches files that don't exist on feat/cursor-rtl-support (e.g., rtl-config.js, README.md, fork-specific things) — that's a sign the fix is mixed with rebranding. Split the commit on main before cherry-picking, so the cherry-pick contains only upstream-relevant changes. On feat, restore feat-branch conventions if needed: getMonacoRTLEnabled uses !== 'false' (not === 'true'); console strings say "RTL for VS Code Agents" (not "& Cursor Agents").
